WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(elevator dings)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.790
(doors scraping)

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:05.720
- Hey, it's me, yeah, Joanna Stern!

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.270
You didn't recognize me?

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.410
You don't like my new look?

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.040
But really, that's me!

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.370
Well, this is me, this is real-life me,

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.120
and that's avatar, virtual-reality me,

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.720
but actually, I'm getting a little tired

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.740
from working from home in the same space,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.556
so I'm gonna go back in here.

00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:23.020
(playful music)

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.067
I think I'll also go here, and here.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.330
Yes, the office.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.850
Sure, when COVID-19 first hit
and we all went into lockdown,

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.930
video calling brought us together.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.270
It was even fun.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.760
But now, the Zoom fatigue is real.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.890
I rarely even look at
my colleagues anymore.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.110
So I've turned to virtual
reality, and as it turns out,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.610
an increasing number of
Fortune 500 companies

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.750
are embracing this technology, too.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.200
It makes sense, as companies have extended

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.130
work-from-home policies until late 2021,

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.660
or even indefinitely,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.850
they're looking at ways to bring

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.920
individuals and teams together.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.690
Plus, even before the pandemic,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.650
virtual reality used within businesses

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.520
was forecasted to grow
from 829 million in 2018

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.733
to 4.26 billion in 2023.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.800
So, a few months ago,
when it became apparent

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.440
we weren't going back to
the office anytime soon,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.440
I started trying it out for myself.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.830
I had a lot of questions.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.760
Can virtual interaction
really mimic the real world?

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.740
What do you need to make it all work?

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.000
Can you really be productive in there?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.890
Will I feel like I need
to barf all the time?

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.040
(groans) Oh boy.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.920
To test it all out, I
worked with a startup

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.750
out of New York City called Spatial,

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.060
to recreate one of my
favorite places in the office.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.159
Yes, the elevator.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:40.229
(elevator dings)
♪ Dance, dance, dance ♪

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:41.450
(fingers snapping)
Okay, it's like this?

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.290
- Don't you really wanna turn
around and face backwards?

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.380
- Yes.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.310
I spend a lot of time in
the elevator in the office.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.970
I have a whole video
interview series in there.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.810
It is an elevator.
- Yeah.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.020
- [Joanna] Okay, fine, spoiler,
it's not a real elevator.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.171
It's a three-wall set.

00:01:55.171 --> 00:01:56.430
- Marking.
(board clacks)

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.610
- Are you Sebastian Thrun?

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.010
Are you Vijaya from Twitter?

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.760
Are you, wait, what are you?

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.570
- I'm a holographic
avatar of Anand Agarawala,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.090
the CEO of Spatial.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.810
- Well, nice to meet
you. Can we shake hands?

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.760
- Yeah!
(elevator music)

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.500
- Wow, wow, look at that!

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.300
We don't even need six feet.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.623
- We can even high-five.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.550
- Wow, so where are we?

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.620
- Spatial is basically a
holographic collaboration platform.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.160
Imagine if Zoom had teleportation.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.710
So you just put on a headset,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.410
and with a realistic avatar generated

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.020
from a simple 2D photo,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.559
you can feel like you're in
the same room with someone.

00:02:36.559 --> 00:02:38.110
(playful music)
- Whoo!

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.770
It's actually kind of crazy
how real it feels in there.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.210
I mean, he does not look

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.000
like him as like a real person,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.470
but his presence feels very real.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.250
But of course, Anand is in
his office in New York City,

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.010
and I'm in my childhood
basement in New Jersey.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.680
My producer, also in a headset
in his home in Brooklyn.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.950
Connecting in Spatial
was really quite easy,

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.370
and anyone can do it.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.700
I signed up for a Spatial
account on the company's website

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.950
and uploaded a photo of myself.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.750
Using some machine learning,
it turned that into my avatar.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.480
Then I downloaded the Spatial app

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.180
on this new $299 Oculus Quest 2.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.060
It works on the older one, too.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.050
It also works on a Microsoft HoloLens

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.990
augmented reality headset,

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.410
oh, which, you know, just costs $3,500.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.243
All right, let's do it.

00:03:26.243 --> 00:03:29.070
You don't need any extra
hardware with those headsets.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.890
All the head and hand tracking just works.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.410
When I move my head, so does my avatar's.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.891
When I move my hands, so does my avatar's.

00:03:36.891 --> 00:03:40.140
Then you can join one of
the spaces Spatial makes.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.470
The company made this
custom elevator for me,

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.320
but the preset Spatial meeting rooms

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.280
are being used by all sorts of companies.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.260
Do you have a sense of
who's using Spatial,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.540
what types of companies?

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.490
- Anywhere from 40% of the Fortune 1000

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.560
to just regular small teams

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:56.800
and people trying to stay connected.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.860
So some of the customers are like Pfizer,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.820
Mattel, the manufacturing company,

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.323
also companies in fashion,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.250
government agencies,
intelligence agencies.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.580
- What about the spaces themselves?

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.820
Are people customizing them to look

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.640
like their real office spaces?

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.180
- Yeah, so that's been
a huge feature request.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.540
We've added a couple more environments

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.720
like a board room and even an abstract one

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.660
where you can overlay your own,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.560
but we are gonna add support for that.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:22.800
- [Joanna] Within the
spaces, you can talk,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.890
but you can also use 3D tools

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:27.040
to brainstorm and look at presentations,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.240
or even manipulate objects, like 3D dogs.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:32.420
- You can virtually pet 'em.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:33.660
- Browser? Where are you?

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.020
Browser? You're gonna get a 3D avatar.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.910
I have a real dog that's much cuter

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.230
than all of these right now.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.510
For real, though, the chief
technology officer at Mattel

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.370
told me employees use Spatial to meet up

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.611
and review 3D models of
toys and store displays.

00:04:46.611 --> 00:04:47.670
(playful music)

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.530
Of course, Spatial isn't perfect,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.000
and certainly misses a
lot of things about a true

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.980
person-to-person office
conversation or elevator ride.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.410
For instance, facial cues
and even body language.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:00.700
Like when I asked,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.410
you've built something pretty cool here.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.429
Has any big company tried
to come and buy Spatial?

00:05:04.429 --> 00:05:05.810
- You know, I mean, it depends,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.870
you know, what is buy, what is it?

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.850
- I've asked that question a
lot of times in this elevator

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.800
and I can usually read
the facial expressions.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.170
But I'm having a hard
time reading right now.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.090
- I'm blushing in real life
and sweating and squirming,

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.640
but you know, I just seem
like cool, calm and collected.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.250
- [Joanna] Then, the tech can sometimes

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.360
get in the way.
(Anand laughing)

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.370
- Buy me a drink first, Joanna, come on.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.630
- Plus, you'll really wanna buy a headset.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.250
Spatial does have a web app,

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.340
and its coming iPhone and Android apps

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.550
let you join in augmented reality.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.450
That is where digital
objects are overlayed

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:38.870
in your real world.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:41.530
But VR is far more immersive.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:44.400
Spatial isn't the only
virtual meeting platform.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.160
Another option, Mozilla Hubs,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.270
has conference-room-like spaces,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.933
though the avatars look pretty cartoonish.

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:53.250
AltspaceVR, owned by Microsoft,

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.140
is used by companies like Accenture

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.890
to hold all-hands meetings.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.480
Facebook, which owns Oculus,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.350
has recently launched its own social

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.489
virtual reality space called Horizon,

00:06:02.489 --> 00:06:05.535
though it's focused on
more playful interactions.

00:06:05.535 --> 00:06:07.950
(quirky music)

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.150
One day, and I really hope one day soon,

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.430
we'll go back to a real office.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.090
Spatial, which was originally designed

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:17.060
to bring colleagues together
from various different offices,

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.670
isn't worried about that, though.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.580
Do you ever think Spatial and VR meetings

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.880
will be as ubiquitous as video calling?

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.150
- 100%, I mean, it's not a question of if,

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.300
it's more a question of when.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.970
There's a near-future where your next

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.290
phone or computer is gonna be glasses,

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:34.920
and in that future, your
FaceTime becomes 3D,

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:36.370
and you know, when you're
FaceTiming your mom,

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.520
she can actually be on
your couch with you.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.300
- That seems a ways off.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.810
For now, though, connecting
with colleagues this way

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.840
really is worth a try.
(Joanna chattering)

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.970
It isn't real life,
but it brings back some

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.720
of the serendipity of the office,

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.190
and goes deeper than just another

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.640
impersonal 2D video call.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.340
And I promise, you can totally
get real work done in there.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.007
(Anand humming)

00:07:03.287 --> 00:07:05.320
(Anand laughing)

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:06.830
Oh no, I don't have legs!

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.363
I don't have a butt or legs!

